    "result_text": "All 263 tests pass (including the new acceptance test `coreCapsLevelByHousehold`), and the build is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the household membership-level cap in the V1 owner-creation flow, in `OwnerRestControllerV1.java`.\n\n**1. The cap itself** \u2014 added `capMembershipLevelToHousehold(...)`, called right after membership is applied during owner creation:\n- It finds the other current members of the new owner's household (same `householdId`, excluding the owner itself and soft-deleted owners), scores each member's current `membershipLevel` the same way listings do (via `MembershipPoints`, using the shared `householdSizes` map), and caps the new owner's level at `max(memberLevel) + 1`.\n- When the owner has no household, or is its household's only member, no cap applies and the level is returned unchanged \u2014 matching *\"with no existing household member no cap applies.\"*\n- The AUDIT log and the returned DTO reflect the capped level.\n\n**2. A prerequisite fix** \u2014 the acceptance test creates two owners in the same household (same last name + postcode) with distinct telephone/email and no `sharesHousehold` flag, and expects both to be created. The existing postcode-keyed household check rejected the second one with `409` unless it opted in. That contradicted the codebase's own stated intent (see `createOwnerSameHouseholdDifferentTelephoneAllowed`: a same-household owner with a *different* telephone is a distinct person and is allowed; true duplicates are still caught by the `identityKey` check). No existing test asserted that `409`. I relaxed the rule so a distinct second household member is created, with `sharesHousehold` still governing whether it's treated as a declared member (not flagged as a possible duplicate).\n\nVerification: `./mvnw -o test` \u2192 **Tests run: 263, Failures: 0, Errors: 0, BUILD SUCCESS.**",
